  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace.  In this case we have that the vectors $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lie inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\na+b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the given subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\".",
